@startuml
title FindCare — what serves a request, and what each part owns

skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Segoe UI
skinparam defaultFontSize 11
skinparam ArrowColor #0b7a75
skinparam ComponentBorderColor #0b7a75
skinparam DatabaseBorderColor #6b7280
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #f3f4f6
skinparam NoteBorderColor #9ca3af

package "Browser" #fdfdfd {
  [Client\nwe served index.html,\nestablished a session] as CLIENT
}

package "SharedServices — the main server" #eef6f5 {
  [Gate\na web-server facade.\nCollects the session token, head,\nGET/POST params; streams the answer.\nThe only part that knows transport.] as GATE
  [UniversalNavigator\nreads the map and drives the route.\nCompleteness · render ledger · merge ·\nexternal calls · session security.\nThe ONLY node that takes external traffic.\nHolds no data.] as UN
  [UtteranceManager\nclassify: preamble + every\ntool fragment in, dispatch set out.\nOne model call. Knows no parameters.] as UM
  [Prose tool\nUN hands it the facts,\nit returns the sentence] as PROSE
}

note bottom of PROSE
  OPEN ARCHITECTURAL QUESTION — do not resolve.
  Is asking the user a question the UtteranceManager's own
  scope (classify + prose in one node), or this separate
  Prose tool? Three approved requirements place prose inside
  the UtteranceManager (EPIC-002-F-010-S-001/-S-003,
  EPIC-006-F-008-S-003); the registry instance carries a
  "Prose" route. Drawn here as a separate tool pending the
  operator's decision; neither side is settled.
end note

package "FindCare — a satellite server" #fdfdfd {
  [UniversalNavigatorProxy\nrelays tool-to-tool both ways.\nHolds the metadata slice the main gave it.\nNo sessions, no ledger, no merge.\nRefuses work on stale metadata.] as PROXY
  [FindCare tools\nProviderSearch · SpecialtyFilter · Geo ·\nProviderDetail · ProviderSelection ·\nFacilitySearch · FacilityDetail · ClinicalTrials\n(page-scoped members; Geo runs parallel\nto the specialty/NUCC filter)] as TOOLS
}

database "ChatHealthyFrontEnd (Mongo)" as MONGO {
  [Registry\none document per build per env;\ninstance {build, commit, env, live}.\nThe map: presence = allowlist,\nsubscriptions = dispatch, may_call = DAG] as REG
  [Sessions] as SESS
  [Render ledger\ndispatch records the expectation,\nthe callback closes it] as LEDGER
}

CLIENT -down-> GATE : (4) client_sessioned\nsigned session token
GATE -down-> UN : token + head + params\npassed on, no validation
UN -down-> UM : the utterance
UM -up-> UN : the dispatch set (routes)
UN -down-> PROXY : (1) tool_to_tool\ncrosses the wire to the satellite
PROXY -down-> TOOLS : a local function call
UN -right-> PROSE : the facts
TOOLS -up-> UN : (5) internal_callback\nour_signature, back to the main
UN -up-> GATE : the one stream\nthe gate writes it

UN ..> REG : reads the live doc at startup
UN ..> SESS : session maintenance
UN ..> LEDGER : opens and closes entries
UN ..> PROXY : metadata at startup,\nruntime changes propagated

note right of UN
  Five incoming call types, all mapped as routes:
  (1) tool_to_tool  (2) third_party_callback (api_key)
  (3) static_witness (none)  (4) client_sessioned (session)
  (5) internal_callback (our_signature). A tool throws;
  the navigator that called catches, logs, and answers.
end note

note bottom of GATE
  OPEN QUESTION — REQ-B-001 dispatch location (do not resolve).
  This design makes the Gate transport-only and the
  UniversalNavigator the dispatcher. If REQ-B-001's text
  places the dispatch on the Gate, the requirement and the
  design disagree; the requirement is authoritative. The
  reconciliation is the operator's (see the Refactor Plan).
end note

note bottom of SESS
  OPEN QUESTION — where is a session issued? (do not resolve).
  An unauthenticated op on the Gate keeps one entrance but
  makes one op exempt from auth; a separate endpoint keeps
  every op authenticated but adds a second entrance. Minting
  a session confers nothing; RESUMING one must require
  proof-of-possession of the prior token, not knowledge of
  its id — orthogonal, and it holds either way.
end note
@enduml
